{# Ward template: .github/copilot-instructions.md for application repositories #}
When reviewing code in this repository, focus on the following areas:

## Code Quality
- Prefer immutability: `val` over `var` in Kotlin, `final` in Java
- Flag commented-out code — it should be deleted, git remembers
- Verify proper error handling: no swallowed exceptions, no empty catch blocks
- Check for resource leaks (streams, connections, response bodies not closed)
- Flag unnecessary abstractions or over-engineering — keep it simple
- Prefer composition over inheritance

## Kotlin Idioms
- Use data classes for DTOs, sealed classes for restricted hierarchies, extension functions where they improve readability
- Flag Java-style patterns: `getInstance()` singletons, unnecessary `!!`, `lateinit` where `lazy` fits better
- Use scope functions (`let`, `apply`, `also`, `run`) appropriately — don't nest them
- Prefer `when` expressions over `if-else` chains for multiple branches
- Use `require()` / `check()` for precondition validation

## Java Patterns
- Use records for immutable data carriers
- Use `var` for local variables when the type is obvious from context
- Use `Optional` return types instead of nullable returns in public APIs
- Lombok: verify `@Getter/@Setter` aren't on classes that should be records
- `@ConfigurationProperties` classes need `spring-boot-configuration-processor` as `annotationProcessor`

## Security
- No secrets, credentials, API keys, or tokens in code — ever
- Validate and sanitize all external input (request params, headers, body)
- Check for SQL injection, XSS, and path traversal vulnerabilities
- Verify authentication and authorization on all endpoints
- Flag overly permissive CORS or Spring Security configurations
- Sensitive data must not be logged (passwords, tokens, personal data)

## Spring Boot
- Constructor injection only — no field injection with `@Autowired`
- Verify proper transaction boundaries (`@Transactional` scope and propagation)
- `@ConfigurationProperties` should use `@Validated` with constraint annotations
- No environment-specific values in base `application.yaml` — use profile-specific files
- Actuator: `/actuator/info` for probes (not `/health` — avoids cascading failures), don't expose sensitive endpoints in production
- Spring Security: use lambda DSL (`.authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> ...)`) not deprecated `.and()` chaining
- `.requestMatchers()` replaces `.antMatchers()`

## Dependencies & Build
- No snapshot dependencies on main branch
- Verify dependency versions are consistent (no duplicate versions of the same lib)
- Gradle: use `./gradlew`, never system Gradle or Maven
- JIB base image should reference the correct JDK version (`eclipse-temurin:{version}-jre`)
- `gradle.properties` should have `org.gradle.parallel=true` and `org.gradle.caching=true`

## Testing
- Tests verify behavior, not implementation details
- Test names describe the scenario, not the method under test
- No test logic or test utilities in production code (`src/main`)
- Verify edge cases and error paths are tested, not just happy paths
- Kotlin: use MockK, not Mockito
- Integration tests: `@SpringBootTest` or `@WebMvcTest` — prefer over extensive mocking

## CI/CD
- Verify workflow actions are pinned to specific versions
- Branch image tags should follow a consistent naming convention
